================================================================================
CASE SOURCE — no steps in AWPTCM or ck.db, AND no TFS exists for this feature
================================================================================
No ck.db case was mapped onto this one. Unlike the other five cases in this campaign, I could
find **no functional spec** for "Extended ID": the lab wiki was searched four ways
("Extended ID TFS", "DHCP Client Extended", "dhcp client-id", "Extended DHCP ID") and returns
nothing for it. So the case is grounded in:

1. **TPS** — wiki "X960_platform_support_TPS" row 30, group "DHCP Client": **Extended ID,
   priority 1, Yes.** ⇒ UNSUPPORTED is not available without DUT evidence, and a TPS "Yes"
   whose command is absent is a FINDING, not a limit.
2. **The X530 Software TPS**, which lists the same DHCP-Client group as
   *Extended ID / DNS Relay / Host Name* — placing "Extended ID" beside "Host Name" as one of
   the client's **identity** options, not as a lease or routing feature.
3. **The DUT's own CLI**, enumerated by read-only help probe BEFORE any test was designed
   (below). The feature is graded against what this build actually offers.
4. **The DUT's own default config**, which already carries on vlan1:
   `ip dhcp-client vendor-identifying-class` and
   `ip dhcp-client request vendor-identifying-specific`.

### The help probe — what this build offers (tools/qmark.py, read-only, no CR)
`qmark.py` types `PREFIX ?` with no carriage return and clears the line with Ctrl-U; its
self-test confirmed first that nothing gets executed ("self-test OK: 'show clock ?' then Ctrl-U
then CR executed nothing"). Never `<cmd> ?` through a CR driver — memory
`never-send-cli-help-through-a-cr-driver`.

    awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp ?
      client-id  Specify the client identifier
      hostname   Specify the hostname
      <cr>
    awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp client-id ?
      IFNAME  Interface name
    awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp hostname ?
      WORD  Hostname for this DHCP client
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client ?
      default-route             Default route
      request                   Request an option
      vendor-identifying-class  Vendor-identifying-class
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client request ?
      vendor-identifying-specific  Vendor-identifying-specific
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client vendor-identifying-class ?
      <cr>
    awplus(config-if)# ip dhcp-client default-route ?
      distance  Set the administrative distance

⇒ The DHCP client's **identity surface** on `sin_x96056-20261012-24` is exactly four things:
option 61 (client-id), option 12 (hostname), option 124 (vendor-identifying class) and the
request for option 125 (vendor-identifying specific). `default-route distance` is routing, not
identity, and is out of scope. **All four were measured on the wire.** TPS row 30 is graded
against that whole surface rather than a guess at which one the title means — which is the
honest treatment when the title has no spec behind it.

