================================================================================
🔴 THE DISCRIMINATOR PROBLEM — settled before any measurement was graded
================================================================================
Flagged at triage as a risk, and it is REAL on this platform:

    show interface vlan1  | include Hardware -> Hardware is VLAN, address is e01a.ead4.7043
    show interface vlan10 | include Hardware -> Hardware is VLAN, address is e01a.ead4.7043
    show interface vlan48 | include Hardware -> Hardware is VLAN, address is e01a.ead4.7043

**All SVIs share one MAC.** `ip address dhcp client-id <IFNAME>` sends "the MAC address
associated with the specified interface", so on this platform the CONTENT of option 61 cannot
distinguish `client-id vlan10` from `client-id vlan48` from any other SVI. Proven, not assumed —
see STEP 2b, where the two produce byte-identical option 61.

⇒ The discriminator used throughout is therefore **presence, type byte and length** of the
option, against its absence in the baseline. Stated here so no reader mistakes the grading for
a content comparison it cannot be.

================================================================================
METHOD — STANDING-ORDERS §7 (scapy, not ping)
================================================================================
A ping cannot read option 61. The DUT is the sender here, so the method is inverted from the
earlier groups: a scapy DHCP server on eth4 answers the DUT and records **every option of
every frame the DUT emits**, with tcpdump on eth4 as the independent witness of what was on the
wire. Each leg changes exactly one configuration item and re-triggers the client with
`shutdown` / `no shutdown` on vlan48; the result is read out of the captured DISCOVER and
REQUEST. The baseline leg, with nothing configured, is the control that every later leg's
option is measured against.

**Count reconciliation (F-3 discipline, applied).** In the `clientid` leg tcpdump saw 7 DHCP
frames while the scapy server reported 3 received. Resolved rather than assumed: the pcap shows
4 client→server frames and 3 server→client Replies; the server processed 3 of the 4 because the
first arrived at 16:46:17, while the server process was still importing scapy (tcpdump starts
first and the import costs seconds — the same cost that produced Group B's H-3). No frame was
lost by the DUT and no count below depends on the gap. **tcpdump is authoritative for what was
on the wire; the server's record is authoritative for what it decoded.**

================================================================================
STEP 1 — BASELINE: plain `ip address dhcp` sends no identity options
================================================================================
  leg `baseline`. COUNTED: 7 DHCP frames on eth4; server decoded DISCOVER + REQUEST.
    DISCOVER chaddr=e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 ip_src=0.0.0.0
        opt param_req_list = [1, 3, 15, 6, 43]
    REQUEST  chaddr=e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43
        opt param_req_list = [1, 3, 15, 6, 43]
        opt requested_addr = 192.168.48.100
        opt server_id      = 192.168.48.2
    leased: e0:1a:ea:d4:70:43 -> 192.168.48.100

  **No option 61, no option 12, no option 124, and no 125 in the request list.** The client
  leases successfully without any of them. This is the control.
  Incidental: the AW+ client's parameter request list is [1, 3, 15, 6, 43] — subnet mask,
  router, domain name, DNS server and vendor-specific. The wiki DHCP page documents only
  1/3/6/15, so option 43 is additional to that page's description. Not graded; noted.
STEP 1 PASS.

================================================================================
STEP 2 — option 61: `ip address dhcp client-id <IFNAME>`
================================================================================
### 2a — the command is REFUSED while a client is already running on the interface
  awplus(config-if)# ip address dhcp client-id vlan10
  % DHCP client is already running on this interface.          (cfg.py exit 2)

  This is a **genuine rejection**: exit 2 AND the state did not change. The working sequence is
  to remove the client first and re-apply with the identifier:
      no ip address dhcp
      ip address dhcp client-id vlan10                          (cfg.py exit 0, no "% " lines)
  **Verified configured before measuring**, not assumed:
      awplus# show running-config interface vlan48
      interface vlan48
       ip address dhcp client-id vlan10
  (The first, refused attempt was re-run correctly before anything was graded. Had it been
  measured as-is, the capture would have shown the DEFAULT client and read as "client-id has no
  effect" — a false defect against TPS row 30. See OBSERVATION O-5 on why this message is not
  the same class as Group B's O-3.)

### 2b — measured
  leg `clientid`, with `client-id vlan10`:
      DISCOVER opt client_id = 01 e01aead47043    (type 0x01 = Ethernet, then the 6-byte MAC)
      REQUEST  opt client_id = 01 e01aead47043
  leg `clientid48`, with `client-id vlan48`:
      DISCOVER opt client_id = 01 e01aead47043    — **byte-identical**

  Against the STEP 1 baseline (option 61 ABSENT), the option appears, well-formed, with the
  RFC 2132 hardware-type byte 0x01 and length 7. The two interfaces give identical content,
  which is the shared-SVI-MAC consequence proven rather than asserted: on this platform the
  `<IFNAME>` argument selects an interface whose MAC is the same as every other SVI's, so it
  changes nothing observable on the wire. That is a property of the platform's MAC allocation,
  not a fault in the command.
STEP 2 PASS (presence/type/length discriminator; content discriminator unavailable, and proven
to be unavailable).

